#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/id #uuid "cd2e9db4-6727-5887-90bc-20eb0a0bf6d2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059441 :question/id #uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" :question/math-academy-id "e-138" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059442 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059443 :question/id #uuid "72830b06-6333-5b61-8257-3967312bd178" :question/math-academy-id "e-140" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = x^4 -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} + x$." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - x^{-2} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} - (-2)x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059444 :question/id #uuid "acfa9025-f984-5b09-9e24-f72707ad67ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-141" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ for $y= 2x^3 + 6\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we convert the radical into a rational exponent, and we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} + 6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3x^{(3 - 1)} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{(1/2 - 1)} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + 3 \\cdot x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061 :topic/id #uuid "d6b26631-d5b7-59f4-92ca-62af4fe95872" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068623 :question/id #uuid "4b9a93ba-ed6c-525f-8930-cb68efd6709e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6085" :question/problem "Find the slope of each of the lines below.\n\n![](images/e9/e901704deb02efe957129512148c97e42b45b4fdd1d0e3af451562cca2ed1181.png)" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the vertical line is undefined. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(4,0)$ and $(4,1),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-0}{4-4}=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the horizontal line is zero. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,3)$ and $(1,3),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{3-3}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15c549c-e56b-5392-a292-e52bc1f58cb0" :knowledge-point/title "Slopes of Horizontal and Vertical Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068669 :question/id #uuid "0b380dbf-80da-582c-a2cd-ab675791c8b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-988" :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48114216e210a4e4dfc353871bbfd16bd2beb480f5831c62189db9299e09f517.png)" :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, we let $(x_1,y_1) = (-2,0)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (0,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-0}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90ce29dd-5214-5ce3-84d0-3f5c304cc7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Graphed Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068670 :question/id #uuid "d086fc71-f668-5ce7-8fd0-b046a3c228eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-989" :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $(x_1,y_1) = (1,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tm &= \\dfrac {y_2 - y_1} {x_2 - x_1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {2 - 5} {4 - 1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {-3}{3} \\\\\n\t\t    &= -1\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=-1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2db034b8-5119-5cf3-bb6e-e6c79d024177" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope Given Two Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068671 :question/id #uuid "7a806653-8d1d-543c-91de-94b9e892b88b" :question/math-academy-id "e-990" :question/problem "Given the following graph, what can we say about the sign of the slope?\n\n![](images/ff/ffa6dd91f72bc66c943e88aa764a64811391a00af162b40320ce3d90f96b16a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($\\searrow$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e9a705e-88d8-58e2-b498-30e3c8e0c26f" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring the Sign of the Slope of a Graphed Line"}] :topic/math-academy-id 396 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050572}] :topic/title "Calculating Slopes of Straight Lines"}]}
